WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.570
Announcer: From the nation's capital, The
American Enterprise Institute for Public Policy

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:18.380
and Research presents "Public Policy Forums,"
a 

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.019
series of programs featuring the nation's
top authorities presenting their differing

00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:26.259
views on the vital issues which confront us.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:29.499
Today's topic, "Revitalizing America: What
are the Possibilities?"

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:36.270
John: In the classic mythology of old Greece
and Homer, ancient mariners feared twin monsters,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.930
Scylla and Charybdis, rock and whirlpool garbed
in the fantasies of the unknown, in the Straits

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.360
of Messina between Sicily and the toe of Italy.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.970
They represented two equally perilous alternatives,
neither of which could be passed without encountering

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:56.080
and probably falling victim to the other.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.260
Mythology deals with the imaginary or fictitious,
with gods and demigods and monsters that dwell

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.320
in the mind.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.060
Those who chart the course of the American
ship of state in this new decade do not deal

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.510
with inventions of the mind.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:17.600
The monsters that they must contend with are
real, facts of daily life, and the way ahead,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.580
a tangle of alternatives so complex and dangerous
as to chill the stoutest heart.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:30.260
A safe course must be plotted in defense,
the economy, foreign affairs, energy, government

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:37.159
regulation, taxes, health, and social and
political processes, and each must succeed

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:40.120
without falling victim to the others.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:45.480
To examine the course to take us to safe harbor,
the way to a revitalized America, we have

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.580
a highly expert panel.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:53.940
To my far right, Congressman Newt Gingrich,
Republican, member of the House Administration,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.490
Public Works, and Transportation committees.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.690
To my immediate right, Representative Richard
B. Cheney, White House Chief of Staff under

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:07.540
President Ford, the new chairman of the Republican
Policy Committee in the House, and a member

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.759
of the Interior and Insular Affairs Committees.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:16.609
To my immediate left, Representative Richard
A. Gephardt, member of the Ways and Means

00:02:16.609 --> 00:02:22.889
and Budget Committees, and appointed Chief
of Task Forces, a new position in Democratic

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:25.329
Party leadership in the house.

00:02:25.329 --> 00:02:32.000
And to my far left, Representative Wyche Fowler,
Democrat, a member of the powerful Ways and

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.409
Means Committee and the Select Committee on
Intelligence.

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:40.450
Well, to begin, gentlemen, I would pose the
same question to each of you.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.790
What are the most critical policy issues,
the major priorities in public policy?

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.930
Representative Cheney.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:52.549
Representative Cheney: Well, I think, John,
that first and foremost from the standpoint

00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:56.659
of the Congress, the issues we're gonna have
to deal with in the months and years ahead

00:02:56.659 --> 00:02:57.849
deal very much with the economy.

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:03.939
It seems to me we've reached a point in this
country where the rate of economic growth

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:08.849
is such that we no longer are able to do either
of two very important things.

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:13.879
One, to provide sufficient public revenues
to take care of our public responsibilities,

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:18.099
not only in the domestic area but also in
terms of national defense.

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:24.599
And secondly, we are no longer able, because
of the lack of vitality in our economy, to

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:31.400
provide a real prospect for the American people,
that their lives of their children will be

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.400
better than their own.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.930
That they can look forward to a bright and
optimistic future situation of economic prosperity

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.170
to come, and we've lost that very, very important
element.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.569
John: Representative Gephardt.

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:46.379
Representative Gephardt: Certainly, one would
have to agree that the economy has to be the

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:48.029
dominant issue.

00:03:48.029 --> 00:03:54.249
Upon it depends most everything in our society
and the economy has simply not been working

00:03:54.249 --> 00:04:00.180
correctly for the past 8 to 10 years, and
therefore the Congress will be dominated by

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.180
that issue.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:06.290
There are lots of sub issues that relate to
it, energy being the most important, and in

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:11.309
my view we will continue to wrestle with that
problem through the rest of our lifetimes.

00:04:11.309 --> 00:04:12.580
John: Representative Gingrich.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.699
Representative Gingrich: I think the two major
issues that are going to affect us in the

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:23.040
whole decade of the '80s are, one, how do
we rebuild a sense of trust and dialogue in

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.730
our political structure, so that people have
some sense that how they vote matters and

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:31.250
that what happens in the elected process means
something?

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:35.130
And second, how do we reopen the system to
new ideas?

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.870
I think that at every level what we're seeing
are symptoms.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:42.569
I think that inflation and the decay of productivity
and the decay of our defense structure, the

00:04:42.569 --> 00:04:47.430
decay of the bureaucracy, in some ways the
collapse of education are all related to the

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:52.289
fact that we're increasingly muscle-bound
in a system that no longer is open either

00:04:52.289 --> 00:04:54.389
to pragmatism or to new ideas.

00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:56.060
John: Representative Fowler?

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:02.150
Representative Fowler: Well, I would certainly
agree with the specifics that my colleagues

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.150
have just mentioned.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:08.949
I would also like to tag on to what my Georgia
colleague, Mr. Gingrich, has said.

00:05:08.949 --> 00:05:15.060
I think that overarching all of our public
policy decisions is the question of how we

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:23.199
once again ensure accountability, and the
public knows where that accountability lies,

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:30.050
where it runs, so that they feel again that
they are part of the system, in a government

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.389
by the governed.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:36.039
The other footnote to that is not talked about
very much.

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:43.080
I think we will see that equally important
as any congressional decision, that more and

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.379
more our lives in this country will be determined,
the quality of our lives in this country will

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:52.919
be determined by the caliber and the quality
of international agreements, equally as much

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:59.229
as domestic legislation, either emanating
from the Congress or from the White House.

00:05:59.229 --> 00:06:00.229
John: Right.

00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:06.389
The National Academy of Public Administration,
a 29-member panel of former cabinet members,

00:06:06.389 --> 00:06:13.530
governors, labor leaders, members of the Congress,
calls for a more strongly united and disciplined

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:19.210
Congress, a greater capacity for coordinated
action by the President and the Congress,

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:25.419
and for a higher level of managerial ability
throughout the government than has been customary.

00:06:25.419 --> 00:06:29.810
How say you to those recommendations as a
prescription for the future?

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.009
Who would like to start it?

00:06:31.009 --> 00:06:33.650
Representative Gephardt: Well, they're good
recommendations.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:38.600
I think I have greater hope for making the
Congress more willing to take on some of the

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:43.690
unpleasant decisions that I think have to
be made in the next 10 years in order to meet

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.919
the problems we've discussed.

00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:53.090
I suppose I'm coming out a bit pessimistic
about our ability to achieve much greater

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.630
levels of competence and managerial ability
in the United States government, the federal

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.330
level.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:04.550
We do not have a system that is a lot like
the Japanese system, where the best and the

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:10.910
brightest desire public service at the bureaucratic
level.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:18.900
We tend to not pay wages that are commensurate
with private industry, certainly today with

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.780
the levels of inflation.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:30.319
So therefore I don't foresee a time when our
executive branch, our bureaucracy is going

00:07:30.319 --> 00:07:32.580
to be improved a great deal.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.039
Jimmy Carter talked about that a lot.

00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:38.600
Most every president talks about it, talks
about reorganization.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.960
We passed a major civil service reform act.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:48.610
It has yielded some good results, but I think
on the whole we can't see much positive happening.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:54.009
I have a greater hope that we will have a
Congress that is more accountable, that's

00:07:54.009 --> 00:07:57.879
more willing to take on some of the tough
decisions that we have to face.

00:07:57.879 --> 00:08:02.569
I think in the past few years, we've seen,
for instance, a budget process which hasn't

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:06.930
yielded a great deal of courage perhaps in
fiscal discipline, but at least we've got

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:12.139
a process in place and hopefully in the years
ahead it will yield that kind of fiscal discipline

00:08:12.139 --> 00:08:13.830
that I think is so important.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:14.919
John: Representative Cheney?

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:17.439
Representative Cheney: Let me differ just
a little bit with Dick.

00:08:17.439 --> 00:08:23.279
I think Congress has been a big part of the
problem, I'd have to argue, and that within

00:08:23.279 --> 00:08:29.749
recent years power has been diffused within
the body, to the extent that Speaker of the

00:08:29.749 --> 00:08:33.820
House, leadership of either party is unable
to deliver.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:39.169
When a commitment's made to the president,
whatever his party might be, it's almost impossible

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:44.600
to build around a commitment by the leader
the kind of coalition that's required to pass

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:45.600
his program.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:51.910
I think that one of the fundamental problems
has been the extent to which we placed restraints

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.230
on presidential authority over the last several
years.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:58.620
We've been so consumed with the trauma of
Watergate and Vietnam that every time we've

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:03.010
tampered with the relationship between the
executive branch and the Congress, we've done

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:10.000
so in a way that's designed primarily to avoid
future abuses of power similar to those that

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.640
are alleged to have happened in the past.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.420
We've been concerned with the so-called Myth
of the Imperial Presidency.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.310
And I don't think there's any way we can address
the public policy agenda that we all talked

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.790
about at the outset unless we really talk
about strong executive leadership, and that

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:28.370
seems to me we've got to restore some balance
between the Congress on one hand and the executive

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:29.370
branch on the other.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.760
John: Representative Gingrich?

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:37.720
Representative Gingrich: If I can dissent
from my good colleague from Wyoming, I think

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:40.330
what we need is a stronger Congress, not a
weaker.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.900
That the greatest danger of the Reagan administration
is that conservatives will decide you can

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:47.760
trust imperial presidents as long as they're
right-wing when they're imperial.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:53.030
That the great problem has been and will continue
to be that we need a generation of social

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:58.420
invention, that the old system has died, whether
it's the big city machine or the Rayburn Congress,

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:02.140
and that in the age of television and jet
aircraft and computerized letters, we have

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:07.360
not yet invented the working political system
that will govern us for the next half century.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.491
But that system, I think, if we're gonna remain
a free country, will be focused in the House

00:10:11.491 --> 00:10:13.150
and the Senate, not in the White House.

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:17.350
And that's one of our major problems is that
for 20 years now, we have focused on the wrong

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:19.470
institution to save this country.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:23.000
And I think that until we get back to that
and things like the invention of the task

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:27.190
force chairmanship that Dick Gephardt has,
I think are steps towards inventing a more

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.530
effective more mobile and more fluid system.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:33.570
Representative Gephardt: Dick, could I follow
on and ask this?

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:34.570
John: Sure.

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:39.370
Representative Gephardt: It's my sense also
that the Congress needs to do a much better

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.060
job of oversight and evaluation.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.930
People have talked for years about sunset
legislation and the Congress doing a better

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:51.540
job of overseeing the implementation of programs
that it's passed.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.090
We aren't doing that yet.

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:54.950
I think we'd all admit that.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:11:00.360
We don't know whether that will ever happen,
but don't you agree that that has to happen,

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.330
or we simply have to keep bumping along the
way we are.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:12.500
We can't really, I don't think, today, ask
Congress to authorize giant programs, to appropriate

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.240
money for those programs, and then to step
back and hope that the executive branch does

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:22.680
what it wants done, without knowing what's
happening, without asking questions, without

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.400
really day-to-day overseeing to a large extent
what is happening.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.920
That doesn't mean they tell them how to run
the department, but it does mean they have

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:35.390
to understand how the law is being implemented
so they can either change the law, or get

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:36.610
rid of it or get rid of the program.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:39.570
Representative Cheney: I certainly would agree
that we need to do a better job on evaluation

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.110
and oversight of the executive branch.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.090
My problem though, and speaking to Newt's
point and follow up on yours too, Dick, you

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.630
serve on the Budget Committee.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:50.380
I think the budget process has become a joke.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:55.440
We're absolutely as an institution incapable
of making the kinds of decisions that the

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:56.550
budget process seems to warrant.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:00.700
I don't think the solution is going to be
found in giving Congress a bigger role than

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:01.700
they've had in the past.

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:05.680
It seems to me a proper way to go would be
for Congress to go ahead and set that overall

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:10.071
spending ceiling, but if we're unable to make
the hard decisions on program cuts, let's

00:12:10.071 --> 00:12:16.010
delegate the authority to the President to
impound funds within certain broad parameters

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:17.060
that we might establish.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:20.930
He used to have that authority, it was used
very effectively, until the Nixon administration,

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:24.320
at which time, because of Watergate, Congress
removed that authority from him.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.690
I don't think the Congress is ever going to
have the courage to do anything about the

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:32.320
entitlement programs, but with strong executive
leadership perhaps we can begin to address

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:33.320
that very fundamental issue.

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:38.570
And you can't talk about addressing the budgetary
problems we face and the economic problems

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.990
we face unless we really are willing to bite
the bullet on entitlement programs.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:44.130
Representative Fowler: I think Dick that you'll
agree the problem with the budget process

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:50.010
is that we all vote for the popular programs,
unwilling to stare a veteran in the face and

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:54.320
say, "No, I'm not gonna double you the benefits
for the 10th straight year."

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:59.870
And go ahead and swell it up and then vote
against the final product that we have swelled

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:04.120
by our own hypocrisy.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:10.680
And I just don't see how turning that back
to the president...First of all, I just don't

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.490
think it's possible under this kind of system.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:15.950
Representative Cheney: Also he's less of a
hypocrite than we sometimes are.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:16.950
John: Representative Gingrich?

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.200
Representative Gingrich: I think the reason
I like the budget system and the fights that

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:24.520
we had in the budget, is that it is the first
real attempt by the house to create a parliamentary

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:29.440
environment in which there is some effort
at some level to say, "Here we stand.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.290
You can measure us on this vote and this has
an aggregate impact on the whole system."

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:39.070
And until you can get to those kind of tough
folks that are an aggregate that you can't

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:42.400
avoid, because unlike Dick, I don't have any
faith in electing presidents who are better

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.400
than Congressmen.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.320
I don't think they are better than Congressmen
[inaudible 00:13:45] frankly.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:48.530
I think it's dangerous to think of presidents
as being more courageous than congressmen.

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:51.680
They just have better speechwriters and better
makeup.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:56.080
I think that the congressmen collectively
are more accurate reflecting the country,

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:00.570
and what you've got to have in my judgment,
is you've got to have a political system that

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:02.760
holds us accountable.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:06.840
And at that point I think somebody's going
to have to bite the bullet even if it takes

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:10.320
three or four cycles of defeat to finally
get to a party that's willing to do it.

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:13.992
Representative Cheney: But Newt, on that point
of accountability, you know, the fact of the

00:14:13.992 --> 00:14:17.090
matter is there's this enormous dissatisfaction
in the country with government.

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.110
You talked about it yourself.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:24.620
Great frustration with the way things are
going, and yet 96% to 97% of all House members

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:25.930
who run for re-election get reelected.

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:26.930
Representative Gingrich: Not this year.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:28.020
Representative Cheney: Well, close to it.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.020
Not that far off.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.960
Representative Gingrich: I mean but when [inaudible
00:14:30] loses and [inaudible 00:14:31] loses

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:33.060
and the Senate changes hands...

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:34.060
Representative Cheney: Okay, all right, but
those are because of special situation.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:38.200
But the basic fundamental long-term average
percentage of House members is well over 90%

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:39.730
who want to get reelected get reelected.

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:44.460
I mean the president of the United States
is the one I think who most accurately reflects

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:46.580
broad fundamental shifts in public opinion.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:47.580
Representative Gingrich: And is the most dangerous.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:50.510
Representative Cheney: I think that's the
most [inaudible 00:14:49] Well, but see if,

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:54.500
you can be concerned about danger, but it
seems to me that in times of great national

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:59.100
peril in the past, whether we talk about the
Civil War, or we talk about FDR during the

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:06.120
depression or World War II, we have basically
responded as a society to those crises by

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:11.560
having the president, whoever he was, assume
authority that was extraordinary, and it seems

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:16.650
to me if you're as concerned as I am about
the 1980s, then we're talking about a situation

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:20.930
again when at a minimum, at a minimum, we
have to reduce the trend of the last few years

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:24.720
where we've undermined presidential authority,
rather than granted him additional authority.

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:28.960
Representative Fowler: Dick, I don't want
to, with none of us, I don't think we want

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:31.270
to come back, keep coming back at you as the
point man...

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:32.270
Representative Cheney: That's all right.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:33.270
Representative Fowler: ...on this.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:38.980
But I just, I find it...I'm just very surprised
about what you said about the president being

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:41.030
the most sensitive to public opinion.

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:42.910
I mean, the United States Congress...

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:45.800
Representative Cheney: In the broad, broad
national opinion, he's far more close to that

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:46.800
than...

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:47.840
Representative Fowler: But see, he doesn't
even have any way of doing that.

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:52.390
I mean he can walk around with a bunch of
polls that may be outdated a week later, depending

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:53.930
on international events.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:59.620
But one of the reasons that Jimmy Carter got
beat, I can say this, coming from Georgia,

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:05.720
is that he did not understand that the people
closest to the people in this country, the

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:10.770
elected officials closest to the people, were
the men and women in the United States Congress.

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:12.340
We are the eyes and ears.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.430
We are the ones that are home every weekend.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:18.600
We are the ones that are on the ground, sitting
and listening to little old ladies telling

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.600
how their Social Security check has just been
eaten alive by inflation.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:25.680
Concerned about immigrants coming into Florida.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:30.610
Worried about people starving across the ocean,
but also having real conflicts as to well

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:33.070
I [inaudible 00:16:31] spend my money when
I don't have any money to do it.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.770
Representative Cheney: But are we capable
of making...

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:35.770
Representative Fowler: And he did not...

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:37.950
Representative Gephardt: Are we capable of
making policy decisions to do anything about

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:38.950
it?

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:40.530
And the answer obviously is no, we can't even
pass a budget.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:45.380
Representative Fowler: Well, see I don't...Well
I would, I, whether that's the overall policy

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:48.160
decision is, I think, up to discussion.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:53.610
But I'm not willing to concede, or we oughta
all go home, that we cannot, along the lines

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:56.620
that Newt was discussing, find a way.

00:16:56.620 --> 00:17:01.010
I'll put...I'll give the other side of the
argument, but giving the same thing that Newt

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:02.010
was saying.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:05.670
Not so much that we have a program that helps
everybody in a certain way.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:10.970
I think that's one of the problems that we've
gotten into, of parceling out the goodies.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:16.530
The American people will support a policy,
in my opinion, if they at least know...if

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:22.720
they know it hurts, but if they know that
everybody is getting hurt equally.

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.980
That everybody is sacrificing.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:31.500
That you're not exempted because you live
on this side of the street, or at a certain

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.430
income, or have a certain special interest
group working for you.

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:39.920
But where we develop a common goal and say,
"Look, it's gonna hurt.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:44.790
But if you want to be energy independent,
if you want to get the inflation down, if

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:51.150
you want to get off of these national deficits,
then we all got to sacrifice in some way."

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:56.030
I don't think it needs to be done in the tone
of a national malaise, as the way President

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:57.880
Carter put it.

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:07.360
But, you know, as a Rooseveltian, Wilson,
Teddy Roosevelt, your Roosevelt, not ours,

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:11.070
policy of how we get from here to there and
what we all have got to do to accomplish it.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.690
Representative Gingrich: The problem I would
suggest to Mr. Cheney here and to people who

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:22.090
look to a president is that the central purpose
of politics in a free society is to be an

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:28.110
arena, in which somebody has a vision, which
they communicate well enough that enough different

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:32.120
interests, in Madison's term, that enough
different interests come together to pass

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:36.910
that vision, and that the gravest danger we
face since Franklin Roosevelt is the perennial

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:43.690
pursuit of a personality that can transcend
by management and administration, the political

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:47.820
job of knitting together a majority in the
House and Senate, and knitting together a

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:51.970
majority in the country and preaching a change
that will help enough people, and it can,

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:56.180
you can help them by stopping inflation, or
you can help them by new opportunities.

00:18:56.180 --> 00:19:01.430
But what worries me precisely about I think
the mindset of the near future, is the danger

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:05.370
that this administration will decide that
while it's conservative in economic philosophy,

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:11.100
it is in fact radically liberal in a willingness
to accrue power, and I think that strong presidents

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:16.210
are inherently and fundamentally dangerous
to this society, because they short-circuit

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:21.070
the political education of saying, "This is
my vision for the '80s.

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:22.340
This is where we have to go.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.310
This is why I want your vote."

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:27.510
And if we don't do that kind of work in the
House and the Senate, and thereby in the country,

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:31.290
because Wyche is right, we're the ones that
go back, then I think we shouldn't have changes

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:33.270
because it'll undermine the whole fabric of
society.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.640
Representative Gephardt: Let me just trail
onto that a moment.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:42.390
I think Jimmy Carter's greatest failing with
Congress was his inability to communicate

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:47.690
effectively and consistently, and on a regular
basis with the members of his own party in

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:49.180
the Congress.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:54.340
I'm new here, I have not seen a lot of congressional
liaison operations.

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:59.720
In my view this was not a good one, because
it did not carry on that kind of consistent

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:00.720
communication.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:05.532
I'm not saying that if it had been done that
a great majority would have fallen in line

00:20:05.532 --> 00:20:10.440
on these tough votes that would have had to
have been taken.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.290
But I agree with Newt that the Congress can
work.

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:18.300
I agree with him that the budget process,
while it has a lot of failings right now,

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:24.470
is a significant step forward, and can really
reach its promise.

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:30.010
But it will take inventive, creative leadership
from the President and a lot of creative leadership

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:35.930
within the Congress, which I think exists
there and can be brought into bloom.

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:40.430
But clearly, it's not there now and maybe
it hasn't been for some time.

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:45.930
John: Let's come down to some of the nuts
and bolts of what you have been putting on

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:50.430
the table here as critical to the formation
of public policy that will give us a more

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.340
confident and constructive future.

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:59.360
Among public policy goals which have been
widely discussed are higher defense spending,

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:02.890
lower inflation, and significant tax cuts.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:07.740
Now can we really achieve any of these goals
in any real near term?

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:12.120
Representative Cheney: I think, John, it's
extremely important when we talk about those

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:15.840
various goals that you have to put them within
some kind of time perspective.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:21.000
I think we've got to talk in terms of a two
or three or four year period of time, and

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:25.510
the bottom line in my mind is that we are
not gonna be able to meet our defense needs,

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:30.010
and we are not going to be able to meet our
domestic needs, unless we do in fact reinvigorate

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.560
the American economy.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:35.990
Without real economic growth, we simply aren't
going to be able to meet our public policy

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:38.870
obligations, let alone take care of the private
sector, too.

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:42.200
Representative Gephardt: I think you have
to reach priorities, I think that's what Dick

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:43.200
is saying.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:48.700
I would put the highest priority on controlling
inflation, because I think short of doing

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:50.960
that, everything else falls apart.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:57.340
I think that takes among other things a restrained
fiscal policy, a creative, effective energy

00:21:57.340 --> 00:22:02.220
policy, and a lot of things happening in the
private sector in terms of productivity and

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:07.230
management-labor relations, and a number of
other things that are not happening at this

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:08.230
time.

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:12.020
I think without addressing that problem as
the number one priority, everything else will

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:13.210
fall away.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:19.530
I think if we can achieve a solution to that
problem, effectively, in the 5 to 10 year

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:24.300
period, which is what I think it will take,
we can then begin to effectively address the

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:26.220
other questions that have been put on the
table.

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:32.840
I think Margaret Thatcher's experience, while
we are not England, is a good thing to look

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:36.980
at in seeing how long it's going to take and
how difficult it will be.

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:38.520
John: [inaudible 00:22:39].

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:45.010
Representative Gingrich: First of all I think
that the major problem in this country economically

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:49.410
is increasing productivity, increasing investment,
increasing new technology.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:53.590
Because I think if you produce enough things
over time they mop up the extra money.

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:58.140
I think that if you try to control inflation
the way we've tried to for the last 20 years,

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:00.510
it's politically impossible in this society.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:05.490
You cannot squeeze inflation out of a democracy,
because in the long run, squeezing it out

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:09.960
squeezes the public, the public won't stand
for it, and if the Republican leadership and

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:15.040
the Reagan administration tried to squeeze
inflation out by severe cuts in spending,

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:20.030
or by severe cuts in terms of monetary policy,
what you are going to see I think is a Democratic

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:21.670
landslide in 1982.

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:27.270
It is not possible, as Margaret Thatcher has
proven, to squeeze a free society in two years

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.430
severely enough to change the inflation rate.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:34.210
What you've got to do instead is change the
tax code, encourage businesses to invest in

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:38.570
new computer systems, new productivity systems,
new factories, so that there's enough extra

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:43.110
productivity that you can both buy the defense
we need, and buy the additional homes and

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:47.050
the additional goodies and pay the taxes I
think to at that point balance the budget.

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:48.050
John: Representative Fowler?

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:50.080
Representative Fowler: That's not going to
work.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:53.860
I say with great respect to my colleague.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:57.940
One of the problems is, of course, I mean
everybody is for increasing productivity in

00:23:57.940 --> 00:23:59.360
certain industries.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:05.980
I think Dick Gephardt and those of us on Ways
and Means, you will see, targeted tax credits,

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:14.380
exemptions, aids for American industry that
will enable them to compete more effectively.

00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:19.210
But the problem with that philosophy is that,
you know, all the world knows how to make

00:24:19.210 --> 00:24:22.309
everything that we make now.

00:24:22.309 --> 00:24:26.240
Used to be that we could just gear up production
and we could make all these things and nobody

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:29.770
else made them, but everybody the world knows
how to make plastic buckets, and electric

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:33.100
toothbrushes, and color television sets.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:35.340
And they're making 'em.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:41.320
And we can produce all we want and find that
there're just not markets for them.

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:47.790
And I of course on the international scene,
part of the balance of payments and the delicacy

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:54.650
of what I mentioned at first of some international
agreements, is a recognition of the fact that

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:58.510
in the production of all these things that
they produce and sell, that they get the foreign

00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:06.420
exchange to buy a lot of our excess wheat,
and many of the monies that come back into

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:07.640
our country.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:15.600
So I would like to think that simply the testing
of that philosophy, you know, if we set the

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:19.710
Keynesians aside, now we're going to go over
and say if we just start producing everything

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:26.650
again, all of a sudden our economy is going
to sing, but I don't see any evidence of that,

00:25:26.650 --> 00:25:31.190
though I think that certainly that will be
the direction that this new Congress goes

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:32.190
in.

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:37.340
John: Well economics are known as the dismal
science, but even there nowadays I think you

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:41.620
tend to hear in these problems you find so
critical in the statement of priorities with

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:48.059
which you opened, this question of the economy,
inflation, and the budget.

00:25:48.059 --> 00:25:54.900
You find in basic economics they talk about
expectations, inflationary expectations perhaps

00:25:54.900 --> 00:26:00.050
more dangerous than any of the specific causes
which bring about inflation, because the public

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:01.460
expects it.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:07.390
Now does the Congress have the means and the
will to try to persuade the American people

00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:10.600
that inflation is going to be brought under
control?

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:14.890
Do you feel that you did it for instance with
respect to energy policy?

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:19.000
How long did it take the American people to
discover we had an energy problem?

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:20.880
And do they have, really understand it yet?

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.880
Representative Gingrich: John, the problem
with that whole line of thinking, frankly,

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:29.230
is that we're a free society, and that people
generally speaking are reasonably bright,

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:32.500
and that they look at all the different indicators
and all the different news shows, and all

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:35.060
the different advertising and they make up
their own minds.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:41.240
And if they look at a country floundering
towards 20% interest rates, they say to themselves,

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:42.940
"Aha, prices are going up."

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:46.490
And they don't care what the Council of Economic
Advisors says, or what the Ways and Means

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:50.570
Committee says, or what the President exhorts
in his latest speech, because they're not

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:51.570
stupid.

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:54.961
And we have had a consistent series of advice
from economists over the years, based on the

00:26:54.961 --> 00:26:58.780
assumption that the public is dumber than
they are, which I think given the track record,

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:00.460
is hard to prove.

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:05.160
You know, I think that we would frankly be
better off literally to ignore...and I say

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:07.920
this as a historian, and maybe have a bias
in that sense.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:11.330
But I think that economics has not only been
the dismal science, it's been the irrelevant

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:12.429
science.

00:27:12.429 --> 00:27:16.010
If we went back to the fundamentals, people
produce more when they get more money for

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:19.630
producing it, they produce less when they
get less money for producing it, and just

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:24.210
ignored most of what we've invented with computers
in the last 20 years in economics, this would

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:27.860
be a far healthier country, and we wouldn't
be trying to outguess the American people.

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:30.560
John: Anybody else want to tackle that one?

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:31.570
Representative Fowler: No, I like that.

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:32.570
John: You like that?

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:35.380
All right, let me come back...Yes, sir?

00:27:35.380 --> 00:27:36.980
Representative Gephardt: I don't like it.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:38.480
John: [inaudible 00:27:38] Good, lay it down.

00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:42.080
Representative Gephardt: I think he's right
when he says they watch what we do rather

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:44.830
than what we say, or what's happening rather
than what we're saying.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:48.610
I don't think you can talk people out of an
inflationary psychology.

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:53.700
I think over a 5 or 10 year period you can
convince them that it's not an inflationary

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:55.309
time.

00:27:55.309 --> 00:27:59.299
I don't go as far as Newt does on what you
can do on the supply side.

00:27:59.299 --> 00:28:00.370
I think it's a good theory.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:05.180
I think we've been hung up on consumption,
and haven't worried about production for too

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:06.180
long.

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:08.450
I just don't go as far as he does.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:14.020
I think you have to balance it with concern
about consumption, and concern about your

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:15.740
fiscal policy.

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:20.020
And I just wouldn't get as carried away as
he does and others in his party do about what

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:21.730
you can accomplish on that score.

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:24.970
But I think there's a great deal of truth
in what he's saying.

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:27.710
John: All right, let's come at it from another
direction.

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:34.150
If we redirect a substantial portion of our
resources to national defense, what specific

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:38.580
areas will we redirect these resources away
from?

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:43.630
Representative Gephardt: Well, there's no
magic in the budget.

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:49.390
If you are for a restrained budget which comes
to as close to balance as you can get with

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:54.790
all the things we want to do, there's only
one place to take the money, and that's from

00:28:54.790 --> 00:29:00.679
domestic programs, and more specifically it
has to come from some entitlement programs

00:29:00.679 --> 00:29:02.520
that Dick talked about.

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:07.730
There are not enough of what we can call controllable,
which is really a misnomer, but everything

00:29:07.730 --> 00:29:09.480
is controllable in the budget.

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:15.330
But we have called a series of domestic programs
that are not Social Security or not disability

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:21.950
payments, are not black lung payments and
what have you as "controllable programs."

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:28.020
There are not enough of those in my view to
cut a budget to get near balance.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:29.720
So you've got to look at Social Security.

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:31.850
You've got to look at disability.

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:36.350
You've got to look at some of the welfare
programs, in addition to health and education

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:41.530
and the other so-called controllable items,
if you're to free up money, significant money,

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:43.309
which I think you have to do for defense.

00:29:43.309 --> 00:29:48.130
Representative Cheney: Yeah, I would point
out I guess if, you know, if you go back to

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:52.340
the peacetime fifties when Dwight Eisenhower
was president, or the early years of the Kennedy

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:56.620
administration when we were at peace as a
country, we spent close to 11% of our gross

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:59.100
national product every year on defense.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:02.900
Nineteen seventy-nine, we spent less than
5% of our gross national product on defense,

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:07.360
and during that same period of time while
we were reducing our overall percentage of

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:11.830
GNP on defense, we went roughly from two dollars
out of every four in the federal budget for

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:17.090
defense, to $1 out of every four, roughly,
for defense.

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:21.100
Simultaneously with that, we diverted resources
into the domestic area.

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:26.090
We adopted the Great Society programs, added
the disability program to Social Security.

00:30:26.090 --> 00:30:29.660
I looked up the numbers the other day on the
disability program.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:34.520
When it was set up in 1956, they estimated
that by 1980 there would be 1 million people

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:37.220
eligible for benefits and it would cost $860
million a year.

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:44.621
The reality in 1980 was that it didn't cost
860 million, it cost $17 billion and there

00:30:44.621 --> 00:30:46.640
were 5 million people involved instead of
1.

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:49.860
There's been an enormous growth in the domestic
side, and I think obviously what you're talking

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:54.350
about is a reallocation of those resources,
not to the extent that we're gonna cut back

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:56.950
below the level we're already spending in
an area.

00:30:56.950 --> 00:31:00.360
But that future growth in the budget is going
to have to come in the defense area, while

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:06.000
simultaneously we put the brakes to the increased
spending in the non-defense area.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.850
And obviously, you're gonna have to address
I think some of the entitlement programs.

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:09.850
John: Representative Gingrich?

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:14.850
Representative Gingrich: John, I, if I can,
comment on one of the premises of your question.

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:18.950
I'm not certain, despite all the rhetoric,
that we need to spend dramatically more on

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:21.620
defense, although I'm a hawk in most ways.

00:31:21.620 --> 00:31:25.450
I am certain that we need to spend our money
differently on defense.

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:29.140
And I think one of the places that the president
ought to be looking at to change the budget

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:31.200
rather than cut it is inside the Pentagon.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:36.380
I think that there is incredible amount of
waste and lack of effective management in

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:37.920
the military.

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:43.640
And I think that we have a set of assumptions
strategically that are nonsense in the present

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:46.960
age, and that we could completely rethink
the way we're doing things.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:52.250
And I have a grave fear that we're going to
in effect spend let's say 8% of GNP instead

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:58.080
of 5%, feel that we are thereby more safe,
and in fact build a Maginot Line behind which

00:31:58.080 --> 00:31:59.230
we accomplish nothing.

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:05.190
I don't see right now any automatic increased
security for America by blindly throwing money

00:32:05.190 --> 00:32:07.290
into the Pentagon and hoping that that scares
the Russians.

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:11.530
Now I think on managing the budget, which
is a different issue, that Ronald Reagan would

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:17.620
do well to study the career of Gladstone,
who spent 40 years being a pinchpenny in Britain,

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:20.920
and convinced the entire middle class that
he looked after their dollars, and that if

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:23.940
he didn't have to spend them in the government
he gave them back to them, so they could spend

00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:25.090
them on their own families.

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:28.799
I think you start frankly with massive decentralization.

00:32:28.799 --> 00:32:33.429
You take virtually every government grant
program, you lump it into five or six categorical

00:32:33.429 --> 00:32:37.480
programs, and you give all that money back
and in a...rather in a block grant, not category.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:40.570
You give all that money back to the state
of Georgia, and you say to the state legislature,

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:44.260
"Figure out how to spend, you know, what we
used to give you in 27 different grants,"

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:50.220
and you eliminate, in this city, I mean, you
have a real estate depression in Washington,

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:53.890
because you shift the bureaucracy out of Washington
and the decision-making out of Washington

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:55.220
back into state and local government.

00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:58.799
I think that alone would probably save us
literally something on the order of $10 to

00:32:58.799 --> 00:33:01.490
$20 billion in an annual budget.

00:33:01.490 --> 00:33:04.260
John: We'd like to hear from the other side
of Georgia.

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:10.250
Representative Fowler: Well, John, first of
all, we have got to in this country come up

00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:12.929
with a redefinition of defense.

00:33:12.929 --> 00:33:19.570
I mean, defense is just not more and more
missiles and more and more technological systems.

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:25.400
Defense is getting off a $90 billion heroin-like
habit on imported energy, which is threatening

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:31.410
our national security because of our profligation
in energy use and its relationship to the

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.760
rest of the world.

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:41.030
Defense is making...Defense is taking some
real risk in foreign policy, that we have

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:45.620
had problems in the Congress reaching any,
even the beginnings of consensus either within

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:47.970
our party or in league with the Republicans.

00:33:47.970 --> 00:33:55.270
I mean when Robert Mugabe, who campaigns as
a Marxist then comes to Washington and says,

00:33:55.270 --> 00:33:58.730
"Look, we have a freely elected government
in Zimbabwe.

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:05.880
We don't want the Russians in there, but we
need your help to the tune of $35, $45 million,

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:09.510
not billion, million, to try to help us keep
the Russians out.

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:10.710
We don't want them.

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:12.129
They just take over your country.

00:34:12.129 --> 00:34:15.309
We want to follow your westernized trends."

00:34:15.309 --> 00:34:20.289
And we shut the door, same in Nicaragua.

00:34:20.289 --> 00:34:25.579
Nobody knows whether or not they're going
to go running to the Russians one year, or

00:34:25.579 --> 00:34:28.379
two years, or two weeks later.

00:34:28.379 --> 00:34:33.609
But the point is you've got to take some risk
around the world to make some friends that

00:34:33.609 --> 00:34:37.359
will be your allies in difficult times.

00:34:37.359 --> 00:34:43.519
We can't...we could spend until the cows come
home, and we could not field an army on the

00:34:43.519 --> 00:34:48.230
Soviet border to protect either Afghanistan
or Poland in my opinion.

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:52.819
We could spend till the cows come home with
all sorts of new technological systems.

00:34:52.819 --> 00:34:58.830
And if we had to draw the line in the Horn
of Africa either in and around Ethiopia, Somalia,

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:00.930
Djibouti, we could not do it.

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:07.440
Because we look at defense spending only in
terms of new Trident submarines, and MX and

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:12.950
all the technological gadgets, and ignore
the rest of the world who are rapidly choosing

00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:17.099
sides to say, "We either want to be on the
side of the United States because they're

00:35:17.099 --> 00:35:22.490
our friend, or we like to go over to the Soviets
because they help us when we need help."

00:35:22.490 --> 00:35:28.479
John: In this whole area of foreign policy,
which I'm glad you raised, and public policy,

00:35:28.479 --> 00:35:34.589
do you see a new direction and perhaps a more
effective and efficient use of public policy

00:35:34.589 --> 00:35:40.249
in the field of foreign affairs in the reaction
to Afghanistan and the Polish crises in 1980?

00:35:40.249 --> 00:35:41.249
I.e.

00:35:41.249 --> 00:35:47.050
an almost acceptance that we are militarily
incapable of acting, but that we will organize

00:35:47.050 --> 00:35:53.730
such economic punishment and public relations
punishment that we could perhaps stop the

00:35:53.730 --> 00:35:54.730
Soviet ambitions?

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:57.229
Do you find this a new approach, an effective
approach Mr. Gingrich?

00:35:57.229 --> 00:36:00.190
Representative Gingrich: John, you've covered
a lot of this over the years.

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:05.319
And there has always been despite the occasional
headlines an American assumption, that we

00:36:05.319 --> 00:36:12.320
had neither the interest, the desire, nor
the will to exert military power within reach

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:14.010
of the Soviet border.

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:18.390
We never under Eisenhower, Kennedy, anyone
thought that we could help Poland.

00:36:18.390 --> 00:36:22.579
Franklin Roosevelt didn't think we could help
Poland, and basically made a deal.

00:36:22.579 --> 00:36:26.530
And that's always been our policy, that Poland
will be as free as the Soviet Union allows

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:28.499
it to be.

00:36:28.499 --> 00:36:34.549
We have no interest in Afghanistan at the
present time, have never had any.

00:36:34.549 --> 00:36:38.079
And I think that you have to look very carefully
at what we want to accomplish with our policy.

00:36:38.079 --> 00:36:44.690
We have defended Europe very ably I think
and very cheaply for 35 years now.

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:49.079
On the assumption that if you keep a large
enough garrison army there, and enough dependents

00:36:49.079 --> 00:36:53.210
there, that a Soviet invasion of Europe means
nuclear war and that there are no risks they'll

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:54.950
take leading to nuclear war in Europe.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:59.750
We've defended Japan under exactly the same
premise, and then on occasion, we have stumbled

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:01.829
into various and sundry secondary wars.

00:37:01.829 --> 00:37:06.970
Vietnam being the least admirable example
both in efficiency and maybe in wisdom.

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:11.510
I think we have to think through our policies,
and we're, you know, we're currently not doing

00:37:11.510 --> 00:37:12.510
that.

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:17.549
What has happened in the last two years, is
the largest swing in public opinion since

00:37:17.549 --> 00:37:18.549
1939.

00:37:18.549 --> 00:37:25.769
What we have seen is a general view, I think
not that we're inferior per se, but that military

00:37:25.769 --> 00:37:27.890
force makes a difference.

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:31.770
And I think in that sense the invasion of
Afghanistan, the Iranian crisis, which I think

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:36.079
had much more to do with our nationalism right
now than Afghanistan, and finally Poland,

00:37:36.079 --> 00:37:41.740
do mean that there's a broad public feeling
that the world is dangerous, and we have to

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:43.470
be capable of surviving in it.

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:45.130
I think that's where we're at.

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:46.130
John: Representative Gephardt?

00:37:46.130 --> 00:37:51.140
Representative Gephardt: Well, I think the
actions taken by the president with regard

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:56.700
to the Soviet Union after Afghanistan were
a departure in public policy, to some extent

00:37:56.700 --> 00:37:58.749
from what we've done in the past.

00:37:58.749 --> 00:38:03.560
It was an exertion of linkage if you will
in the Soviet relationship.

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:07.970
It was an attempt to treat their bad behavior
with punishment.

00:38:07.970 --> 00:38:10.549
I think it had an effect.

00:38:10.549 --> 00:38:13.049
Will it stop them from going into Poland?

00:38:13.049 --> 00:38:15.460
Probably not, but did it have an effect?

00:38:15.460 --> 00:38:19.269
I think it did and I think it was a constructive
effect.

00:38:19.269 --> 00:38:24.400
And I think we all however agree that having
sufficient military power as one of our foreign

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:28.940
policy tools is absolutely necessary, and
that since Vietnam, whether we like it or

00:38:28.940 --> 00:38:33.210
not, we've allowed that military machine to
fall into a certain state of disrepair, which

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:34.210
has to be remedied.

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:39.680
John: All right, I think that we have opened
up this whole issue of the possibilities of

00:38:39.680 --> 00:38:40.680
future.

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:45.119
We've certainly decided the possibilities
for revitalizing America are numerous, but

00:38:45.119 --> 00:38:49.109
that the achievement of the goals is going
to be a hard road, and it's time for us to

00:38:49.109 --> 00:38:50.410
have a question-and-answer session.

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:52.599
May I have the first question please?

00:38:52.599 --> 00:38:53.970
Howard: I'm Howard Pennyman.

00:38:53.970 --> 00:38:58.890
I'm from American Enterprise Institute in
Georgetown University.

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:05.319
Could I go back to some of the very early
discussion?

00:39:05.319 --> 00:39:10.180
The question of whether people represent their
district or represent the United States is

00:39:10.180 --> 00:39:11.180
an old question.

00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:16.289
Woodrow Wilson said as far as a century ago
that American congressman represented their

00:39:16.289 --> 00:39:19.829
district and not the nation as a whole.

00:39:19.829 --> 00:39:29.700
Every basic reform, whether it be to change
the electoral system or to reform the Congress

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:34.900
has tended to further fragment and decentralize
the Congress.

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:39.319
One of the results is, it seems to me, and
I'd like to ask all of you to comment on it,

00:39:39.319 --> 00:39:47.720
is that it has so made congressman represent
their districts that they both A, believe

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:54.450
deeply in cutting the budget, and B in never
cutting it where it would hurt anybody among

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:57.010
those who have interests in their districts.

00:39:57.010 --> 00:39:59.130
John: Right, who would like to start with
that?

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:03.539
You look like a good, bold, brave...To Cheney.

00:40:03.539 --> 00:40:09.440
Representative Cheney: I think most of us
attempt to achieve some kind of balance.

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:13.330
I mean after you've campaigned for a year
or two years or in the case of Mr. Gingrich

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:21.219
from Georgia, three separate times before
you win, you feel that you have a common identity

00:40:21.219 --> 00:40:22.799
with your constituents.

00:40:22.799 --> 00:40:26.249
And I think most members probably when it
comes time to cast a vote on a key issue,

00:40:26.249 --> 00:40:32.783
rarely feel a tug between, on the one hand,
so-called national interest and on the other

00:40:32.783 --> 00:40:33.783
hand, district interest.

00:40:33.783 --> 00:40:37.180
You've rationalized in your own mind that
what your constituents want and what you want

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:38.849
is what's right for the country.

00:40:38.849 --> 00:40:43.400
It's those fellows on the other side that
are representing the special interest in their

00:40:43.400 --> 00:40:47.140
districts, so that the choice in the mind
of the congressman I think is really framed

00:40:47.140 --> 00:40:48.450
in those clear-cut terms.

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:54.400
Representative Gephardt: I think your attitude
toward voting is in large part dependent upon

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:57.059
your last majority.

00:40:57.059 --> 00:41:05.999
If you won by 50.00001%, I think you keep
your ear pretty close to the ground in preparation

00:41:05.999 --> 00:41:07.970
for the next campaign.

00:41:07.970 --> 00:41:15.720
If you won by 95% of the vote, I think you
vote fairly freely according to the dictates

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:21.579
of your conscience, whether that be in line
with your district, and what it thinks and

00:41:21.579 --> 00:41:25.890
what the majority thinks, or what would be
seen as a national position.

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:34.619
Howard: [inaudible 00:41:27] congressman Cheney's
position about giving authority to the President

00:41:34.619 --> 00:41:36.749
to carry out the cuts which you talk about.

00:41:36.749 --> 00:41:41.800
Representative Gingrich: Let me make a comment,
because it...I don't know...Political science

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:46.359
has done an enormous disservice to government
in this country, because it is almost completely

00:41:46.359 --> 00:41:47.900
ahistorical.

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:52.359
The founding fathers deliberately designed
one branch of the government to be permanently

00:41:52.359 --> 00:41:54.950
scared, and that's why they have them run
every two years.

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:57.529
It is a deliberate design function.

00:41:57.529 --> 00:42:02.509
They specifically want us to speak for the
district, and they have the rather naive belief

00:42:02.509 --> 00:42:08.440
that if a president can't find 218 districts
that gain more than they lose from a particular

00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:10.609
bill, that that bill ought to be defeated.

00:42:10.609 --> 00:42:13.920
Now I think by George, you're supposed to
speak for the people of your district, because

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:18.670
that's who you understand, that's who you
represent, and that you collectively, as The

00:42:18.670 --> 00:42:20.960
House, represent the country.

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:24.420
And I think any congressman who gets up and
says, "Well, this will destroy my district

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:28.680
economically, but in the national interest
I want to do that," is in fact betraying their

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:31.109
specific commitment in the Constitution.

00:42:31.109 --> 00:42:38.130
And I find it very frankly naive and destructive
in the long run to keep favoring a general

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:42.700
good which has no specific good, because I
don't think there is a general good for the

00:42:42.700 --> 00:42:46.130
country as a whole, which you can't piece
together in 218 districts.

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:49.440
Representative Cheney: But Newt wouldn't you
agree then on that basis, and I think this

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:54.279
goes back to the question that was asked,
that given the constitutional arrangement,

00:42:54.279 --> 00:43:00.970
given our mandate as congressmen to represent
our districts, that in looking for that spark,

00:43:00.970 --> 00:43:05.719
that dynamic spark or leadership or initiative
that is going to set the agenda and help solve

00:43:05.719 --> 00:43:07.430
the problems, we have to look to the presidency.

00:43:07.430 --> 00:43:11.760
That that is more likely to be the arena in
which the answers are found than in the Congress.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:14.579
Representative Gingrich: It is specifically
and precisely in the Federalist Papers and

00:43:14.579 --> 00:43:18.710
in almost all the writing of the founding
fathers, a concern that we have effective

00:43:18.710 --> 00:43:23.180
leadership in the Congress, because it is
among the congressmen that you should make

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:27.839
the compromises and the understandings and
the level of political sophistication necessary

00:43:27.839 --> 00:43:32.729
both to manage the people who we pay, the
bureaucrats, and to rearrange the legislation

00:43:32.729 --> 00:43:33.729
for our constituents.

00:43:33.729 --> 00:43:37.969
And it is the failure of our generation to
produce effective political and congressional

00:43:37.969 --> 00:43:42.630
leaders in the Congress that leads us I think
to the very grave danger in the long run of

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:43.630
tyranny.

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:44.700
John: We would be most interested to hear
from you, Mr. Fowler.

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:48.010
Representative Fowler: Thank you Mr. Speaker.

00:43:48.010 --> 00:43:54.359
Well, I think it may be naive and I will admit
to that.

00:43:54.359 --> 00:44:00.059
But to respond to Mr. Gingrich.

00:44:00.059 --> 00:44:06.170
What we need in the United States Congress
are men and women who are not afraid to lose.

00:44:06.170 --> 00:44:16.739
Who do not see this as a lifelong profession
to service a constituency so readily, and

00:44:16.739 --> 00:44:23.710
only that constituency in every little whim
of public policy that the breeze may blow,

00:44:23.710 --> 00:44:26.599
to simply perpetuate them in office.

00:44:26.599 --> 00:44:32.529
Now I know that it's a lot easier to say than
to do, if they're going to close a military

00:44:32.529 --> 00:44:37.130
base in your district, to build a more modern
one somewhere else.

00:44:37.130 --> 00:44:40.479
That is in the national interest to do so.

00:44:40.479 --> 00:44:44.719
It is not in the economic interest of that
member's district.

00:44:44.719 --> 00:44:46.430
That's when you earn your pay.

00:44:46.430 --> 00:44:54.130
To vote to close it in your district in order
to achieve the national interest to modernize

00:44:54.130 --> 00:44:58.420
your defense establishment.

00:44:58.420 --> 00:45:04.809
I mean these questions have been debated,
but we are not, 535 men and women, are not

00:45:04.809 --> 00:45:07.050
city councilmen.

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:09.009
We're not state legislators.

00:45:09.009 --> 00:45:16.109
We shouldn't be ward politicians catering
to whatever sense of information and purpose

00:45:16.109 --> 00:45:21.410
that comes on a weekly basis, or you will
never mold any national policy.

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:24.680
But the problem is that we won't stay here.

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:31.430
So most of us are afraid to vote on any given
issue in something that's too brave, because

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:33.069
then we get thrown out next time.

00:45:33.069 --> 00:45:35.109
Representative Gephardt: Can I go back on
it?

00:45:35.109 --> 00:45:39.730
I think the question is perhaps not as well
put as it could be.

00:45:39.730 --> 00:45:46.210
Most issues don't break down very neatly,
as I think Dick said, into a district interest

00:45:46.210 --> 00:45:48.400
and a national interest.

00:45:48.400 --> 00:45:55.229
It breaks down between what you perceive as
being both in the interest of your district

00:45:55.229 --> 00:46:00.809
and the national interest, and an interest
group that is usually represented in your

00:46:00.809 --> 00:46:03.759
constituency.

00:46:03.759 --> 00:46:08.740
Let's take the example of the twice a year
COLA for federal retirees, the cost of living

00:46:08.740 --> 00:46:10.210
adjustment.

00:46:10.210 --> 00:46:16.410
I think most members probably felt that that
was a change.

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:21.869
Making it a once a year cost of living adjustment
was a change that was in order, that was legitimate

00:46:21.869 --> 00:46:23.440
and appropriate.

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:29.010
But each of us had 3 or 5 or 6,000 federal
retirees sitting in our district, writing

00:46:29.010 --> 00:46:31.380
letters, making telephone calls.

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:37.170
And so people, a lot of people before an election,
did not want to vote against that large group,

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:41.319
when you're winning by 1,000 or 3,000 or 6,000
votes.

00:46:41.319 --> 00:46:46.339
That to me is the way a lot of issues are
presented, and I don't think it breaks down

00:46:46.339 --> 00:46:48.970
as neatly as perhaps your question suggests.

00:46:48.970 --> 00:46:50.930
John: The next question please.

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:51.930
Yes sir?

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:57.530
John H.: I'm John Hellman [SP], I'm from the
University of Washington in Seattle, and AEI.

00:46:57.530 --> 00:47:02.079
And I, relaying a question as chairman of
a Department of Political Science from my

00:47:02.079 --> 00:47:03.670
students.

00:47:03.670 --> 00:47:08.719
Because I've been quite amazed to hear four
congressmen talk about revitalizing America

00:47:08.719 --> 00:47:13.910
without directly addressing a question of
revitalizing Congress.

00:47:13.910 --> 00:47:19.960
The mood, if I may repeat my students, is
that Washington is a subculture, that congressmen

00:47:19.960 --> 00:47:27.289
do not act as statesmen, they simply represent
those interests that have access to them,

00:47:27.289 --> 00:47:31.359
be they local constituents or Washington interest
groups.

00:47:31.359 --> 00:47:34.150
They rarely transcend the immediate.

00:47:34.150 --> 00:47:38.200
They do not provide a basis for leadership
for the country.

00:47:38.200 --> 00:47:44.780
I call to your attention also repeated public
opinion polls expressing approval which rarely

00:47:44.780 --> 00:47:51.119
exceeds 30% of the performance of the role
of Congress, and one taken in the early, I

00:47:51.119 --> 00:47:56.390
guess, about 1974, where congressmen were
rated above trash collectors and below barbers

00:47:56.390 --> 00:47:58.650
in terms of professional respect.

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:03.890
I would like to raise the question to all
of you.

00:48:03.890 --> 00:48:06.480
What is wrong?

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:12.440
And do you think anything can be done to alter
the image of Congress?

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:18.489
Because the perception out there is not that
you are doing a superb job, but that somehow

00:48:18.489 --> 00:48:22.539
you are doing a job that must be done but
not doing it very well.

00:48:22.539 --> 00:48:24.170
John: Mr. Fowler has volunteered.

00:48:24.170 --> 00:48:29.359
Representative Fowler: Well, let me let me
wade in where angels fear to tread for your

00:48:29.359 --> 00:48:35.180
students, to say that first I would say to
your students that in a free society, in a

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:40.550
representative system, that you get the kind
of government that you deserve.

00:48:40.550 --> 00:48:44.940
If you tolerate apathy in your public officials,
you get apathy.

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:48.579
If you tolerate corruption, then you get corruption.

00:48:48.579 --> 00:48:54.440
Because we are still a government by the governed,
by those students and the people in this room

00:48:54.440 --> 00:48:56.920
and people throughout the country.

00:48:56.920 --> 00:49:03.140
The Congress will never be any greater in
its statesmanship than the values and the

00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:07.680
hopes and the demands of the American people.

00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:13.609
We have the same faults, they are certain
because we are representative, and come from

00:49:13.609 --> 00:49:16.200
a system where we are elected every two years.

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:22.059
We mirror those hopes and those aspirations,
and those faults.

00:49:22.059 --> 00:49:24.519
That means you get a certain number of blue-eyed
people.

00:49:24.519 --> 00:49:25.700
You get a certain number of alcoholics.

00:49:25.700 --> 00:49:31.529
You get a certain number of any other trend
that you want to put on your computer.

00:49:31.529 --> 00:49:38.589
But back to the question that we discussed
before, the real question is in a pluralistic

00:49:38.589 --> 00:49:45.119
society, where you've got black people and
Spaniards and rich people and poor people.

00:49:45.119 --> 00:49:49.450
How on any given vote can you be truly representative?

00:49:49.450 --> 00:49:55.109
I represent a district that's 50% black and
50% white.

00:49:55.109 --> 00:50:00.329
A district where the gap between the number
of high-income people and number of low-income

00:50:00.329 --> 00:50:05.979
people, that gap in between is greater than
any other congressional district in the country

00:50:05.979 --> 00:50:09.279
according to all these computer operators.

00:50:09.279 --> 00:50:15.390
So the question that I would ask to your students
is how can you be on any given vote truly

00:50:15.390 --> 00:50:16.450
representative?

00:50:16.450 --> 00:50:18.059
You can't.

00:50:18.059 --> 00:50:22.930
I know that on any given vote, I'm not going
to make 50% of them mad, but probably 80%

00:50:22.930 --> 00:50:28.269
of them mad, when I cast a vote that I deem
to be in the national interest.

00:50:28.269 --> 00:50:36.210
But you have to turn this question back in
a free society, to the people who are the

00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:39.009
electorate, because they are the government
in this country.

00:50:39.009 --> 00:50:45.049
And again, they're going to get what they
deserve in their representatives in Washington.

00:50:45.049 --> 00:50:51.660
Representative Cheney: I think that the Congress
itself has to bear a great deal of the blame

00:50:51.660 --> 00:50:55.480
for the way government's perceived, because
we probably do as much communicating as anybody

00:50:55.480 --> 00:51:01.180
in America today next to Walter Cronkite and
the people on the evening news shows.

00:51:01.180 --> 00:51:02.430
We send out newsletters.

00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:03.560
We make speeches.

00:51:03.560 --> 00:51:05.819
We visit with our constituents.

00:51:05.819 --> 00:51:10.119
To the extent that the American people have
a perspective on government, I think to some

00:51:10.119 --> 00:51:12.009
extent we're responsible for having created
that.

00:51:12.009 --> 00:51:16.739
We create it by castigating the courts, and
condemning the bureaucracy, and worrying about

00:51:16.739 --> 00:51:20.480
alleged abuses of presidential power by presidents.

00:51:20.480 --> 00:51:26.720
And we also I think have failed in the very
important perspective of being reflective

00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:28.940
about what our role and our responsibility
is.

00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:30.630
Yes, we do have to be representative.

00:51:30.630 --> 00:51:34.079
Yes, we do have to pass appropriations bills
and conduct oversight.

00:51:34.079 --> 00:51:36.259
But we don't debate the great issues of the
day.

00:51:36.259 --> 00:51:43.109
We spend hours and hours and hours on absolutely
idiotic procedural questions.

00:51:43.109 --> 00:51:48.319
We don't get into the basic fundamental issues
that I think the American people often feel

00:51:48.319 --> 00:51:54.209
ought to be the central concern of their government,
and to some extent we brought it on ourselves.

00:51:54.209 --> 00:51:58.430
Representative Gephardt: I think your students
are probably basically right.

00:51:58.430 --> 00:52:04.809
I think that there are two instances or two
conditions which will change the condition

00:52:04.809 --> 00:52:05.809
of Congress.

00:52:05.809 --> 00:52:10.250
One is if more of your students will take
it upon themselves to run for Congress, rather

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:14.150
than going into the business world to make
a big buck.

00:52:14.150 --> 00:52:18.950
Or secondly if we have a catastrophe in the
country and people really begin to worry about

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:20.359
what Congress is doing.

00:52:20.359 --> 00:52:24.369
As Dick said, 95% of us get reelected.

00:52:24.369 --> 00:52:30.130
If we're not exerting leadership, then the
American people don't want leadership, because

00:52:30.130 --> 00:52:34.250
they're reelecting people that your students
say are not giving them leadership.

00:52:34.250 --> 00:52:38.619
I think as long as things were going along
relatively well, and they have been for 40

00:52:38.619 --> 00:52:43.820
or 50 years up until about the last 10, nobody
really cared.

00:52:43.820 --> 00:52:48.630
Representative Gingrich: [inaudible 00:52:45]
both of you, because you're both Democrats

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:51.829
and just went through a tough election in
terms of your party at large.

00:52:51.829 --> 00:52:57.660
It seems to me that one of our great problems
is that each of us is individually perceived

00:52:57.660 --> 00:52:59.789
as a very effective representative.

00:52:59.789 --> 00:53:05.349
That the problem is that Congress in the aggregate
is not able to function in, it's an anti-synergism,

00:53:05.349 --> 00:53:06.480
whatever that word is.

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:11.569
That we bring together each of the 435 parts
and we somehow become less than the sum of

00:53:11.569 --> 00:53:12.569
the whole.

00:53:12.569 --> 00:53:13.630
And I think that's been one of our great problems.

00:53:13.630 --> 00:53:16.500
We're all much better as individuals than
we are as a team.

00:53:16.500 --> 00:53:19.219
Representative Fowler: You know, I was running
around in my district and I looked at this

00:53:19.219 --> 00:53:21.569
high school that I used to go to, a public
high school.

00:53:21.569 --> 00:53:24.210
I couldn't figure out how it was different.

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:25.339
It looked different.

00:53:25.339 --> 00:53:29.760
I could tell, but I couldn't...it took me
20 minutes of looking at it.

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:36.849
And all of a sudden I realized that it was
this one tremendous parking lot around it.

00:53:36.849 --> 00:53:41.640
We didn't have cars 20 years ago when I went
to high school.

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:44.730
Now everybody's got their own car.

00:53:44.730 --> 00:53:48.739
So when your students start worrying about
whether or not, why we don't have an effective

00:53:48.739 --> 00:53:55.740
energy policy in this country, ask them how
many of them would be willing to be statesmen,

00:53:55.740 --> 00:54:03.809
and give up those cars until we got our imported
oil bill down from $90 billion a year to $10

00:54:03.809 --> 00:54:05.749
billion a year?

00:54:05.749 --> 00:54:07.979
And we'll test statesmanship at both ends.

00:54:07.979 --> 00:54:16.060
Representative Gephardt: But Wyche, I sense
that your students are asking for tough leadership

00:54:16.060 --> 00:54:18.500
decisions, like a gas tax.

00:54:18.500 --> 00:54:21.219
John Anderson was very popular on college
campuses.

00:54:21.219 --> 00:54:24.640
He talked about tough solutions to tough problems.

00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:28.269
And I think what you're saying is that that's
what they're asking for.

00:54:28.269 --> 00:54:35.079
And I would just suggest back that that will
happen when either we have a catastrophe,

00:54:35.079 --> 00:54:39.660
and then people demand those kinds of things
happen, are willing to have a consensus form

00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:40.890
in that regard.

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:47.709
Or until we get 219 people in the house and
51 people in the Senate who perceive that

00:54:47.709 --> 00:54:52.009
that kind of thing, I'm not saying that's
necessarily a right policy, but those kinds

00:54:52.009 --> 00:54:53.710
of things are necessary to do.

00:54:53.710 --> 00:54:55.699
Representative Fowler: You should go ahead
and endorse my policy.

00:54:55.699 --> 00:54:58.459
Representative Gephardt: I'm for the gas tax.

00:54:58.459 --> 00:54:59.459
I voted for it.

00:54:59.459 --> 00:55:00.459
John H.: One final remark.

00:55:00.459 --> 00:55:02.369
The students want to believe.

00:55:02.369 --> 00:55:04.829
I think that is an interesting point.

00:55:04.829 --> 00:55:11.380
Enrollments in political science are up 50%,
60% over the last two years.

00:55:11.380 --> 00:55:17.130
And the interest in governance and governmental
institutions is truly growing.

00:55:17.130 --> 00:55:22.859
And Anderson's appeal was very substantial
around altruistic symbols.

00:55:22.859 --> 00:55:29.800
I think the complaint about Congress is they
don't find inspiration, they don't find something

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:36.230
there that they can look to as more than a
mirror of the society.

00:55:36.230 --> 00:55:41.079
Maybe it can't be more than that but that's
somehow what they want.

00:55:41.079 --> 00:55:43.170
John: Anybody else wants to talk to this?

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:44.369
Representative Gingrich: [inaudible 00:55:44]
thought.

00:55:44.369 --> 00:55:49.640
What if in fact the reason we are floundering
around in the Congress is because the country

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:51.420
is floundering around?

00:55:51.420 --> 00:55:57.299
What if in fact it is the nature of a free
society to take long periods to talk to itself,

00:55:57.299 --> 00:56:02.839
to sort things out, to move slowly so as not
to crush any single group, and that one of

00:56:02.839 --> 00:56:05.799
the things that our students are going to
have to learn in the long run is that to be

00:56:05.799 --> 00:56:11.319
free there's on occasion to be impatient,
to be frustrated and have to work awfully

00:56:11.319 --> 00:56:15.549
hard to get anything done, because that is
in fact precisely what freedom takes.

00:56:15.549 --> 00:56:20.029
I think in that sense, if you look at our
record compared to Nazi Germany or compared

00:56:20.029 --> 00:56:25.440
to Soviet Russia, we don't do all that bad
a job over 20 years, although certainly we

00:56:25.440 --> 00:56:27.400
do a terrible job over 1 year.

00:56:27.400 --> 00:56:32.599
John: All right, this concludes another public
policy forum presented by the American Enterprise

00:56:32.599 --> 00:56:35.510
Institute for Public Policy Research.

00:56:35.510 --> 00:56:41.339
On behalf of AEI, our hearty thanks to the
distinguished and expert panelists, Congressmen

00:56:41.339 --> 00:56:47.700
Newt Gingrich, Richard B. Cheney, Richard
Gephardt, and Wyche Fowler, and of course,

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:50.190
also to our guests and experts in the audience
for their participation.

